window.SLIDE_TEXT["cad-part-5"] = {"1":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease Part V:</li><li>Pharmacologic Management of Ischemic Heart Disease and ACS</li><li>PHAS 517</li><li>Jessica Stager, MPAS, PA-C</li><li>November 19, 2026</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Ischemic Heart DiseaseDeterminants of Oxygen Supply and Demand</li><li>Oxygen Supply</li><li>Oxygen Demand</li><li>Coronary blood flow</li><li>Arterial oxygen content (hemoglobin and oxygen saturation)</li><li>Coronary perfusion pressure</li><li>Heart Rate</li><li>Contractility</li><li>Ventricular wall stress</li></ul>",
"3":"<ul><li>Alteration of Oxygen Supply/Demand</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Increased Demand</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Non-Cardiac</td><td>Cardiac</td></tr><tr><td>Hyperthermia (Fever) Hyperthyroidism Sympathomimetic toxicity (Meth, cocaine) Hypertension Anxiety Beta agonists (Albuterol)</td><td>Tachycardia Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy Aortic stenosis Increased contractility</td></tr><tr><td>Decreased Supply</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Non-Cardiac</td><td>Cardiac</td></tr><tr><td>Anemia Hypoxemia Sickle cell disease Sympathomimetic toxicity Increased viscosity / hemoconcentration (polycythemia vera, severe dehydration)</td><td>Coronary Artery Disease Aortic stenosis Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</td></tr></table>",
"4":"<table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Signs and Symptoms Associated with Angina</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>Pressure, heavy weight, burning, tightness, SOB, deep, gradual</td></tr><tr><td>Radiation</td><td>Left arm, left shoulder, jaw, right arm, back</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>2 to 30 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Precipitating factors</td><td>Exertion, cold temperatures, fright, anger, sexual intercourse</td></tr><tr><td>NTG relief?</td><td>Within 45 seconds to 5 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Substernal pain radiating to left shoulder/upper arm, lower jaw, lower cervical or upper spine, left/interscapular/suprascapular, rarely right arm</td></tr></table>",
"5":"<ul><li>Treatment Goals in Coronary Artery  Disease</li><li>Short-term:</li><li>Reduce or prevent the symptoms of angina that limit a patient’s exercise capability and impair their quality of life</li><li>Restore balance between supply and demand</li><li>Long-term:</li><li>Prevent CAD events such as myocardial infarction, arrhythmias and heart failure</li><li>Prevent morbidity and mortality</li></ul>",
"6":"<ul><li>Treatment in a Nutshell</li><li>Reduce future cardiovascular risk through lifestyle changes and evidence-based preventive medications.</li><li>Relieve angina and improve function with antianginal medications, selected according to symptoms, heart rate, blood pressure, and comorbidities.<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Calcium channel blockers</li><li>Nitrates</li><li>Ranolazine</li></ul></li></ul>",
"7":"<ul><li>Risk Factor Modification: Smoking, Hypertension, Lipids</li><li>Tobacco: Assess use, advise cessation, and offer behavioral support and medication when appropriate. Encourage avoidance of secondhand smoke.</li><li>Blood pressure: Treat hypertension; a goal below 130/80 mm Hg is appropriate for many patients with CAD, individualized to the patient.</li><li>Lipids: Use guideline-directed lipid-lowering therapy—typically high-intensity statin therapy for established ASCVD—and reassess LDL-C response to determine whether additional therapy is indicated.</li></ul>",
"8":"<ul><li>Risk Factor Modification: Exercise, Weight, Diabetes, Alcohol</li><li>Physical activity: Aim for at least 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity, as tolerated; tailor activity to symptoms and clinical status.</li><li>Weight: Encourage sustainable weight management and assess weight-related health risks; BMI is one measure, not a universal treatment target.</li><li>Diabetes: Individualize glycemic goals. For many nonpregnant adults, an A1C goal around 7% is reasonable, but goals depend on comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, and patient factors.</li><li>Alcohol: Do not recommend alcohol for cardiovascular benefit. If patients drink, discuss risks and moderation based on their health and medications.</li></ul>",
"9":"<ul><li>Stable Angina</li><li>Nitrates</li><li>Beta-Blockers</li><li>Calcium Channel Blockers</li><li>Ranolazine (Ranexa)</li></ul>",
"10":"<ul><li>Drug Therapy for Stable Angina</li><li>There are three classes of anti-ischemic drugs commonly used in the management of chronic stable angina:<ul><li>Beta blockers<ul><li>Nitrates</li><li>Calcium channel blockers</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Choose therapy based on heart rate, blood pressure, ventricular function, comorbidities, and response.</li><li>A beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker is commonly used initially; additional therapy may be needed if symptoms persist.</li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>Nitrates</li><li>Mechanism of Action:</li><li>Nitrates are converted to nitric oxide, which increases vascular smooth-muscle relaxation.</li><li>Predominant effect: venodilation → decreased venous return and preload.</li><li>Reduced preload lowers ventricular wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand.</li><li>Nitrates also dilate coronary arteries and can relieve coronary vasospasm.</li></ul>",
"12":"<ul><li>Nitrates</li><li>Oxygen Supply</li><li>Oxygen Demand</li><li>coronary vasodilation may improve flow, particularly with coronary spasm</li><li>↓ preload →</li><li>↓ ventricular wall stress → ↓ myocardial oxygen demand</li></ul>",
"13":"<table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td></td><td>IV NTG</td><td>SL NTG</td><td>Isosorbide Dinitrate</td><td>NTG patch</td><td>Isosorbide mononitrate</td></tr><tr><td>Onset of Action</td><td>1-2 min</td><td>1-3 min</td><td>20-40 min</td><td>40-60 min</td><td>30-60 min</td></tr><tr><td>Duration of Action</td><td>3-5 min</td><td>30-60 min</td><td>4-6 hours</td><td>&gt; 8 hours</td><td>6-8 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Initial Dose</td><td>5-10 mcg/min</td><td>0.4 mg PRN</td><td>10-20 mg TID</td><td>1 patch</td><td>30 mg daily</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrate Tolerance</td><td>YES</td><td>YES</td><td>YES</td><td>YES</td><td>YES</td></tr><tr><td>Indications</td><td>Acute CP, HTN</td><td>Acute CP</td><td>Stable angina</td><td>Stable angina</td><td>Stable angina</td></tr></table>",
"14":"<ul><li>IV Nitroglycerin</li><li>Can be used for persistent ischemic discomfort despite sublingual therapy, once the IV is established.</li><li>The drip can be titrated up or down and the effect is transient. This is useful in patients with marginal blood pressure</li><li>Also, the treatment of choice for hypertensive emergency associated with decompensated HF/pulmonary edema</li></ul>",
"15":"<ul><li>Sublingual Nitroglycerin</li><li>Taken on a PRN basis: for quick relief when CP occurs. All patients with CAD are instructed to carry NTG tablets with them.</li><li>Directions for use:<ul><li>Stop activity and sit down.</li><li>Take one sublingual nitroglycerin dose at the onset of chest pain.</li><li>If pain is not relieved within 5 minutes, call 911. Do not wait to finish all three doses.</li><li>While awaiting EMS, additional doses may be taken every 5 minutes, up to three total, if blood pressure permits and the patient has been instructed to do so.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"16":"<ul><li>Sublingual Nitroglycerin</li><li>Special Considerations:<ul><li>Headache is a common adverse effect of nitroglycerin. Results from vasodilation, including dilation of cerebral blood vessels.</li><li>SL nitro causes rapid venous and to a lesser extent, arterial vasodilation.<ul><li>Sit down before placing the medication under the tongue and allowing it to dissolve</li></ul></li><li>Do NOT swallow the tablet! - let it dissolve slowly</li></ul></li></ul>",
"17":"<ul><li>Long-Acting Nitrates</li><li>Long-acting nitrates are the second-line medication when a patient has frequent anginal symptoms that are not controlled with a beta blocker.</li><li>Isosorbide mononitrate (Imdur)<ul><li>Extended release is given once daily</li><li>Immediate release is given at 8am and 3pm to allow for a drug free interval overnight.</li></ul></li><li>Isosorbide dinitrate (Isordil): Given 2-3 times daily in a similar fashion.</li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>Long-Acting Nitrates</li><li>Extended-release nitrates are used for prevention of anginal symptoms, not acute relief.</li><li>Dosing is scheduled to provide coverage during periods when the patient is most likely to experience angina.</li><li>A daily nitrate-free interval (typically 10–12 hours) is needed to minimize nitrate tolerance.</li></ul>",
"19":"<ul><li>Side Effects of Nitrates</li><li>1. Tolerance (Tachyphylaxis)</li><li>Decreased responsiveness to nitrates with continuous or prolonged exposure.</li><li>Can develop within 24–48 hours of continuous nitrate therapy.</li><li>A daily nitrate-free interval helps maintain effectiveness.</li><li>2. Headache</li><li>Common adverse effect due to vasodilation.</li><li>May occur shortly after administration.</li><li>Does not indicate whether the medication is therapeutically effective.</li></ul>",
"20":"<ul><li>Side Effects of Nitrates</li><li>3. Flushing</li><li>Results from vasodilation.</li><li>4. Orthostatic hypotension / Dizziness</li><li>Due primarily to venous pooling and decreased venous return.</li><li>Sit down before taking short-acting sublingual nitrates.</li><li>Use caution in patients with low blood pressure or volume depletion.</li></ul>",
"21":"<ul><li>Nitrate Contraindications</li><li>Drug-Drug Interactions: PDE-5 inhibitors which are prescribed for erectile dysfunction and pulmonary hypertension.<ul><li>Sildenafil (Viagra or Revatio)<ul><li>Vardenafil (Levitra)</li><li>Tadalafil (Cialis, Acarida)</li></ul></li><li>If a nitrate is used within 24 hours of a PDE-5 inhibitor, severe hypotension may develop.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>Nitrate Contraindications and Cautions</li><li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with LV outflow tract obstruction: Nitrates reduce preload and may worsen obstruction and symptoms.</li><li>Severe aortic stenosis: Use cautiously because vasodilation and reduced preload may cause hypotension.</li><li>Volume depletion or low blood pressure: Nitrates may worsen hypotension.</li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>Nitrates</li><li>Monitoring:<ul><li>Monitor blood pressure, heart rate, dizziness, and anginal symptoms.</li><li>Track use of short-acting nitroglycerin and the frequency or pattern of chest discomfort.</li><li>Short-acting nitroglycerin is for acute episodes or predictable exertion.</li><li>New, worsening, prolonged, or rest symptoms require urgent evaluation.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>Nitrates</li><li>Be aware that nitrates can be used off-label to relieve esophageal spasm- a differential when a patient presents with chest pain.</li><li>So, substernal pain that resolves with nitro can be of esophageal origin!</li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers</li><li>β1 receptors — Located primarily in the heart and juxtaglomerular cells of the kidneys.</li><li>Stimulation increases heart rate, contractility, AV nodal conduction, and renin release</li><li>Blockade reduces these effects</li><li>β2 receptors — Present in bronchial and vascular smooth muscle, among other tissues.</li><li>Stimulation promotes bronchodilation and vasodilation</li><li>Blockade may cause bronchoconstriction</li></ul>",
"26":"",
"27":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers</li><li>First-line therapy in the control of symptoms in patients with chronic stable angina.</li><li>Beta-blockers block beta-adrenergic receptors, reducing the effects of epinephrine and norepinephrine on the heart.</li><li>They reduce myocardial oxygen demand primarily by decreasing HR, BP and contractility</li><li>Lower heart rate also increases diastolic filling time, which may support coronary perfusion.</li></ul>",
"28":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers</li><li>β1-selective (cardioselective): Preferentially block β1 receptors; examples include metoprolol, atenolol, and bisoprolol.</li><li>Nonselective: Block both β1 and β2 receptors; examples include propranolol, nadolol, and timolol.</li><li>β-blockers with additional α1 blockade: carvedilol, labetalol</li></ul>",
"29":"<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Oxygen Supply</li><li>Oxygen Demand</li><li>May support coronary perfusion bylengthening diastolic filling time</li><li>Main antianginal effect:↓ Heart rate</li><li>↓ Contractility</li><li>↓ Blood pressure/wall stress</li></ul>",
"30":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers</li><li>Avoid in vasospastic angina</li><li>Beta-blockers—particularly nonselective agents—may worsen coronary vasospasm due to unopposed alpha-receptor activity</li><li>Calcium channel blockers are preferred first-line therapy for vasospastic angina; nitrates may also be used</li></ul>",
"31":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers: Adverse Effects</li><li>General: Fatigue, dizziness, reduced exercise tolerance, cold extremities, sexual dysfunction.</li><li>Cardiovascular: Bradycardia, hypotension, AV block; may worsen acute decompensated HF.</li><li>Respiratory: Bronchospasm (especially nonselective agents).</li><li>Metabolic: May mask adrenergic warning signs of hypoglycemia (e.g., tachycardia), especially in patients using insulin or sulfonylureas.</li><li>Withdrawal: Abrupt discontinuation may cause rebound tachycardia, angina, or MI; taper gradually.</li></ul>",
"32":"<ul><li>Beta-Blockers: Contraindications</li><li>Severe bradycardia or symptomatic hypotension</li><li>Second- or third-degree AV block without a    functioning pacemaker</li><li>Acute decompensated heart failure, cardiogenic shock, or low-output state</li><li>Active bronchospasm</li></ul>",
"33":"<ul><li>Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs)</li><li>Calcium channel blockers (CCBs) are a first-line option alongside beta blockers for chronic stable angina.</li><li>They relieve symptoms by reducing myocardial oxygen demand, increasing coronary blood flow, or both.</li><li>Calcium Channel Blockers are categorized as dihydropyridine (DHP) or non-dihydropyridine (Non-DHP)</li></ul>",
"34":"<ul><li>Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs)</li><li>DHP CCB: Block calcium entry in vascular smooth muscle, leading to arterial vasodilation and reduced BP/afterload<ul><li>Amlodipine</li><li>Nifedipine</li></ul></li><li>Non-DHP CCB: Block calcium entry in the cardiac tissue, which helps slow heart rate, decrease contractility and control arrhythmias<ul><li>Verapamil</li><li>Diltiazem</li></ul></li></ul>",
"35":"<ul><li>Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs)</li><li>CCB should be considered if there are contraindications or adverse reactions to beta blockers/nitrates or if symptoms are not well controlled with a combination of these agents</li><li>DHP CCB, such as amlodipine, is recommended when used in combination with a beta-blocker</li><li>Combining a beta blocker with a non-DHP CCB can cause excessive bradycardia, AV block, or worsening LV dysfunction</li><li>For vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina, CCBs are preferred; beta blockers may worsen coronary spasm.</li></ul>",
"36":"<ul><li>Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs)</li><li>Adverse Effects:</li><li>DHPs (amlodipine, nifedipine ER):<ul><li>Edema</li><li>Headache</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Reflex tachycardia, more likely with short-acting agents (Nifedipine)</li></ul></li></ul>",
"37":"<ul><li>Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs)</li><li>Adverse Effects:</li><li>Non-DHPs (verapamil, diltiazem):<ul><li>Bradycardia and AV block</li><li>Hypotension, dizziness, and fatigue</li><li>Worsening heart failure in patients with reduced LV systolic function</li><li>Constipation, especially with verapamil</li></ul></li></ul>",
"38":"<ul><li>Ranolazine (Ranexa)</li><li>MOA: inhibits the late phase of inward sodium channels in cardiac myocytes during repolarization reducing intracellular sodium concentration and thereby reducing calcium influx<ul><li>Prolongs the ventricular action potential</li><li>Reduces ventricular wall tension, improves O2 delivery</li><li>Decreases myocardial oxygen demand</li><li>Ranolazine does NOT affect the HR and BP!</li></ul></li></ul>",
"39":"<ul><li>Ranolazine (Ranexa)</li><li>Ranolazine is an add-on antianginal for patients who remain symptomatic despite a beta blocker, calcium channel blocker, or long-acting nitrate.</li><li>Use and dosing:<ul><li>Start 500 mg extended-release twice daily; increase to 1,000 mg twice daily if needed and tolerated.</li><li>With diltiazem or verapamil, limit ranolazine to 500 mg twice daily because these drugs increase its concentration.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"40":"<ul><li>Ranolazine (Ranexa)</li><li>Adverse Effects:<ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>QT prolongation</li></ul></li><li>Extensive hepatic metabolism. Contraindicated in patients with liver cirrhosis/clinically significant hepatic impairment.</li><li>Monitor renal function in patients with moderate-to-severe renal impairment.</li></ul>",
"41":"<ul><li>Ranolazine (Ranexa)</li><li>Drug-Drug Interactions:<ul><li>Digoxin: Increases digoxin levels; monitor and adjust dose as needed.</li><li>Simvastatin: Maximum dose 20 mg/day.</li><li>Diltiazem / Verapamil: Limit ranolazine to 500 mg twice daily.</li><li>Strong CYP3A inhibitors: Avoid (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin).</li><li>Grapefruit juice / Fluconazole: Limit ranolazine to 500 mg twice daily.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"42":"",
"43":"",
"44":"<ul><li>Acute Coronary Syndromes</li><li>Unstable Angina</li><li>Non-ST Elevation MI (NSTEMI)</li><li>ST Elevation MI (STEMI)</li></ul>",
"45":"<ul><li>Treatment Goals</li><li>Relieve ischemic discomfort and reduce myocardial oxygen demand.</li><li>Restore coronary blood flow when indicated and limit myocardial injury.</li><li>Prevent further thrombus formation and recurrent ischemic events.</li><li>Reduce complications and mortality.</li></ul>",
"46":"<ul><li>Drug Therapy – Acute Coronary Syndrome</li><li>Core ACS therapies: aspirin, a P2Y12 inhibitor, and an anticoagulant; reperfusion when indicated.</li><li>Symptom-directed therapies: nitroglycerin, beta-blocker, and oxygen when clinically indicated.</li><li>Selected situations: opioids for persistent pain despite anti-ischemic treatment; fibrinolysis for eligible STEMI when timely PCI is unavailable.</li><li>Avoid routine use: non-aspirin NSAIDs; prophylactic antiarrhythmics.</li></ul>",
"47":"<ul><li>Nitroglycerin</li><li>Mechanism: predominantly venodilation → ↓ preload and ventricular wall stress → ↓ myocardial oxygen demand.</li><li>SL nitro at first, often given by EMS, then IV drip if pain persists</li><li>Avoid with hypotension, suspected right ventricular infarction, or recent PDE-5 inhibitor use.</li><li>Use caution in severe aortic stenosis or obstructive HCM.</li></ul>",
"48":"<ul><li>Oxygen</li><li>Give supplemental oxygen if oxygen saturation &lt;90% to improve myocardial oxygen supply and decrease anginal symptoms</li><li>Patients without hypoxia have not demonstrated any clinical benefit from routine use of supplemental oxygen</li></ul>",
"49":"<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Administration in ACS can reduce the size of the infarction by improving perfusion</li><li>Decreases recurrent ischemia or re-infarction</li><li>Decreases mortality when continued long term</li><li>Decreases the incidence of lethal ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death</li></ul>",
"50":"<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Oral beta blocker should be administered within the first 24 hours to patients without contraindications.</li><li>A cardioselective beta blocker is preferred in the setting of an acute MI (metoprolol, atenolol).</li><li>There is no apparent benefit to giving the beta blocker prior to PCI as long as the beta blocker is begun within the first 24 hours after the patient presents.</li></ul>",
"51":"<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Titrate the dose to a goal HR of 55-70 bpm while maintaining a systolic BP &gt; 90mmHg</li><li>Contraindications to beta blockers in ACS<ul><li>Overt signs of decompensated heart failure</li><li>Bradycardia &lt;55 bpm</li><li>Hypotension with a BP less than 90 mmHg systolic, cardiogenic shock</li><li>Second or third-degree heart block</li><li>Active asthma, bronchospasm, wheezing</li><li>Reassess once the patient is clinically stable.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"52":"<ul><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Long term: base decision on EF and other indications</li><li>Reduced EF or heart failure: Continue beta-blocker therapy unless contraindicated<ul><li>For chronic therapy when LVEF is below 50%,  guideline-preferred options include metoprolol succinate, carvedilol, or bisoprolol</li></ul></li><li>Preserved EF, uncomplicated/revascularized MI: Routine lifelong therapy has not shown clear outcome benefit; reassess the need over time.</li></ul>",
"53":"<ul><li>ACEi/ARB</li><li>ACE-inhibitors used post-MI can reduce mortality and recurrent cardiovascular events</li><li>The 2025 ACS guideline recommends an oral ACE inhibitor—or an ARB if an ACE inhibitor is not tolerated—for patients with ACS who have any of these high-risk features:<ul><li>LVEF ≤40%</li><li>Heart failure</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Diabetes</li><li>Anterior STEMI</li></ul></li><li>Modest survival benefit even in lower-risk MI patient</li></ul>",
"54":"<ul><li>ACEi/ARB</li><li>Typically started within the first 24 hours of ACS presentation</li><li>Continued long-term for high-risk patients or those with reduced EF</li><li>Monitor blood pressure, creatinine, and potassium after initiation and dose changes</li><li>Avoid ACEi + ARB combination</li></ul>",
"55":"<ul><li>Analgesia - Opioids</li><li>Consider IV morphine or fentanyl for severe pain that persists despite maximally tolerated anti-ischemic therapy.</li><li>Not routine first-line treatment; do not use simply to mask ongoing ischemia.</li><li>May cause hypotension, respiratory depression, nausea, and sedation.</li><li>Can slow gastric emptying and delay the effect of oral P2Y12 inhibitors.</li></ul>",
"56":"<ul><li>Analgesia - Opioids</li><li>Monitor:<ul><li>Pain relief</li><li>Vitals: BP, RR</li><li>CNS/respiratory depression</li><li>Pruritus may occur from opioid-related  histamine release and does not always indicate allergy</li></ul></li><li>Reversal agent: Naloxone (Narcan®) 0.4 mg IVP.  Should be on hand or easily accessible</li></ul>",
"57":"<table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Medication</td><td>Route</td><td>Suggested Dosing</td><td>Considerations</td></tr><tr><td>Nitroglycerin∗</td><td>SL (tablets, spray)</td><td>0.3 or 0.4 mg every 5 min as needed up to a total of 3 doses</td><td>Use in hemodynamically stable patients with SBP ≥90 mm Hg.</td></tr><tr><td>Nitroglycerin∗</td><td>IV</td><td>Start at 10 μg/min and titrate to pain relief and hemodynamic tolerability.</td><td>Consider for persistent anginal pain after oral nitrate therapy, or if ACS is accompanied by hypertension or pulmonary edema.20-22 Avoid use in suspected RV infarction, SBP &lt;90 mm Hg or a change in SBP &gt;30 mm Hg below baseline. Tachyphylaxis may occur after approximately 24 h.</td></tr><tr><td>Morphine</td><td>IV</td><td>2-4 mg; may repeat if needed every 5-15 min. Doses up to 10 mg may be considered.</td><td>Use for relief of pain that is resistant to other maximally tolerated anti-ischemic medications. May delay the effects of oral P2Y12 therapy.7,9-12 Monitor closely for adverse effects.</td></tr><tr><td>Fentanyl</td><td>IV</td><td>25-50 μg; may repeat if needed. Doses up to 100 μg may be considered.</td><td>Use for relief of pain that is resistant to other maximally tolerated anti-ischemic medications. May delay the effects of oral P2Y12 therapy.8 Monitor closely for adverse effects.</td></tr></table>",
"58":"<ul><li>Analgesia</li><li>Avoid non-aspirin NSAIDs (such as ibuprofen or diclofenac) for suspected or confirmed ischemic chest pain when possible</li><li>NSAIDs are associated with increased cardiovascular events and have no established routine role in ACS analgesia</li></ul>",
"59":"<ul><li>Antiplatelets vs Anticoagulants vs Fibrinolytics</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Medication type</td><td>What it does</td><td>Simple way to remember</td><td>Examples</td></tr><tr><td>Antiplatelets</td><td>Inhibit platelet activation or aggregation</td><td>Prevent the platelet plug from growing</td><td>Aspirin, clopidogrel, ticagrelor, prasugrel</td></tr><tr><td>Anticoagulants</td><td>Inhibit coagulation factors and fibrin formation</td><td>Slow formation of the fibrin mesh</td><td>Heparin, enoxaparin, bivalirudin</td></tr><tr><td>Fibrinolytics</td><td>Promote breakdown of fibrin in an existing clot</td><td>Dissolve the clot’s mesh</td><td>Tenecteplase, alteplase</td></tr></table>",
"60":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents</li><li>Aspirin: COX-1 inhibitor</li><li>P2Y₁₂ inhibitors:<ul><li>Clopidogrel — thienopyridine</li><li>Prasugrel — thienopyridine</li><li>Ticagrelor — non-thienopyridine</li></ul></li></ul>",
"61":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - ASA</li><li>Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid)</li><li>Available in oral tabs of 81, 162, 325 and 500 mg or as a 300mg rectal suppository</li><li>MOA:</li><li>Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1.</li><li>This decreases thromboxane A₂ production and reduces platelet aggregation.</li><li>Its antiplatelet effect lasts for the life of the affected platelet.</li></ul>",
"62":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - ASA</li><li>At presentation with ACS:</li><li>Give 162–325 mg non–enteric-coated aspirin, chewed, as soon as possible unless contraindicated.</li><li>Give the loading dose even if the patient takes daily aspirin.</li><li>Follow with 81 mg daily.</li><li>Aspirin plus an oral P2Y₁₂ inhibitor is dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT).</li></ul>",
"63":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - ASA</li><li>After MI (secondary prevention):</li><li>For most patients with ACS who are not at high bleeding risk, the default is DAPT—aspirin plus a P2Y₁₂ inhibitor—for at least 12 months</li><li>After that, low-dose aspirin is commonly continued long term for secondary prevention</li></ul>",
"64":"<ul><li>Sunil V. Rao et al. JACC 2025; 85:2135-2237.</li><li>2025 American College of Cardiology Foundation, and the American Heart Association, Inc.</li></ul>",
"65":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - Clopidogrel (Plavix)</li><li>Used in ACS and in selected patients with coronary artery disease.</li><li>Also used for secondary prevention after ischemic stroke and for symptomatic peripheral artery disease.</li><li>Loading dose in ACS: 300-600mg</li><li>Maintenance dose: 75 mg daily</li><li>Rare adverse effect: thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)—requires urgent recognition and treatment.</li></ul>",
"66":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - Prasugrel (Effient)</li><li>Indications<ul><li>ACS patients undergoing PCI (STEMI or NSTEMI), when appropriate.</li></ul></li><li>Dosing:<ul><li>Loading dose: 60 mg orally when PCI is planned</li><li>Maintenance dose: 10 mg orally daily</li><li>Consider 5 mg daily for patients weighing &lt;60 kg</li></ul></li></ul>",
"67":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents - Prasugrel (Effient)</li><li>Adverse Effects:</li><li>Bleeding: Most important adverse effect; may be severe or life-threatening. Monitor for signs of bleeding and easy bruising.<ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a history of stroke or TIA</li><li>It is generally avoided in patients aged 75 or older, except in selected high-risk patients.</li></ul></li><li>Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP): Rare  but serious; may present with fever, weakness, thrombocytopenia, renal dysfunction, or neurologic changes.</li></ul>",
"68":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents- Ticagrelor (Brilinta)</li><li>Indications<ul><li>ACS patients managed with PCI or medical therapy</li></ul></li><li>Dosing:<ul><li>Loading dose: 180 mg orally<ul><li>Maintenance: 90 mg twice daily for the first 12 months after ACS</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"69":"<ul><li>Anti-Platelet Agents- Ticagrelor (Brilinta)</li><li>Adverse effects:</li><li>Bleeding: Most important adverse effect; monitor for signs of bleeding.</li><li>Hyperuricemia: May increase serum uric acid; use caution in patients with a history of gout.</li><li>Dyspnea: Usually mild to moderate and often resolves with continued therapy.<ul><li>Evaluate other causes of dyspnea (e.g., HF, recurrent ischemia) before attributing it to ticagrelor. Consider \tan alternative if symptoms are intolerable.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"70":"<ul><li>Important Warnings &amp; Precautions:</li><li>Aspirin dose: Use maintenance aspirin 75–100 mg daily (typically 81 mg). Doses above 100 mg/day may reduce ticagrelor effectiveness.</li><li>Administration: Tablets may be crushed and mixed with water for patients unable to swallow.</li><li>Anti-Platelet Agents- Ticagrelor (Brilinta)</li></ul>",
"71":"<ul><li>Key Takeaways:</li><li>Antiplatelets target the platelet plug</li><li>Anticoagulants target the fibrin mesh</li><li>In ACS, they are complementary and are usually used together (e.g., aspirin + P2Y₁₂ inhibitor plus heparin)</li></ul>",
"72":"<ul><li>Anticoagulant Therapy</li><li>Anticoagulation helps prevent thrombus propagation and recurrent coronary thrombosis during ACS treatment.</li><li>Options include:<ul><li>Unfractionated heparin (UFH) as an IV drip titrated with PTT or anti-Xa levels</li><li>Low-molecular weight heparin (LMWH) - therapeutic dose enoxaparin (Lovenox), 1 mg/kg SQ q12 hours</li></ul></li></ul>",
"73":"<ul><li>Anticoagulant Therapy</li><li>Unfractionated heparin (UFH) or low-molecular weight heparin are used in patients with NSTEMI and STEMI and should be started in the ER as soon as the MI is identified.<ul><li>Enoxaparin (Lovenox) is given only if the patient is not anticipated to go to LHC.</li><li>Unfractionated heparin drip is preferred in STEMI or NSTEMI with plans for LHC.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"74":"",
"75":"<ul><li>NSTEMI/Unstable Angina: Invasive or Selective Strategy?</li><li>Invasive strategy: coronary angiography with revascularization when appropriate.</li><li>Selective invasive strategy: medical treatment initially, with angiography if recurrent ischemia, instability, or other indications develop.</li><li>Decide based on overall risk and clinical context—not a single score alone.</li></ul>",
"76":"<ul><li>Provide guideline-directed anti-ischemic and antithrombotic therapy as appropriate.</li><li>Monitor symptoms, ECG, hemodynamics, and evidence of recurrent ischemia.</li><li>Obtain echocardiography when indicated to assess ventricular function and complications.</li><li>Reconsider angiography if symptoms recur or the patient becomes unstable.</li><li>Selective Invasive Strategy: Medical Management</li></ul>",
"77":"<ul><li>Invasive Strategy / STEMI</li><li>Contact cardiology/interventional cardiology promptly when an invasive strategy is indicated.</li><li>Give aspirin and other antithrombotic therapy according to the ACS pathway.</li><li>Use nitrates and beta-blockers only when clinically appropriate and not contraindicated.</li><li>Do not delay urgent angiography or reperfusion to achieve pain relief or complete medication administration.</li><li>STEMI requiring primary PCI: activate the cath lab and proceed as rapidly as possible.</li></ul>",
"78":"<ul><li>NSTEMI: Stable vs. Unstable</li><li>Unstable NSTEMI (e.g., ongoing/refractory ischemia, hemodynamic instability, life-threatening arrhythmia): urgent invasive evaluation.</li><li>Stable NSTEMI: determine timing of angiography from overall ischemic risk and clinical course.</li><li>Use antithrombotic and anti-ischemic therapy as appropriate.</li><li>NPO status should be tied to planned procedures.</li></ul>",
"79":"<ul><li>PCI</li><li>Indications in ACS:<ul><li>Patient with STEMI [door to PCI time &lt;90 min]</li><li>High risk NSTEMI patients</li></ul></li><li>Requires a PCI capable facility</li><li>A PCI utilizes contrast dye to visualize the vessels during the LHC procedure<ul><li>May lead to anaphylactoid reactions: Pretreat with steroid or antihistamine if a concern</li><li>Potential for renal failure: Pre-hydration is key!<ul><li>IV Normal Saline, consider N-acetylcysteine PO BID x 4 doses, Hold metformin 48 hours prior to/after LHC.  Hold other nephrotoxins.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"80":"<ul><li>Invasive Treatment: CABG</li><li>CABG = coronary artery bypass graft</li></ul>",
"81":"<ul><li>Fibrinolytics: Alteplase (tPA)</li><li>MOA: Activates plasminogen which accelerates the conversion of plasminogen to plasmin to break down fibrin.</li><li>Alteplase has been proven beneficial in limiting infarct size, improving LV function and reducing mortality, but it is far inferior to PCI- therefore it is only used when a PCI cannot be done.</li><li>Alteplase is only used for a STEMI.</li></ul>",
"82":"",
"83":"<ul><li>Fibrinolytics: Alteplase (tPA)</li><li>Use: A patient presents within 12 hours of CP onset with significant ST elevation MI and an invasive strategy is NOT an option.</li><li>Patients who are given a fibrinolytic should be on UFH/LMWH/fondaparinux for minimum of 48 hours and up to 8 days after the drug.  This helps to prevent small thromboses from causing additional blockages as the clot is lysed.</li></ul>",
"84":"<ul><li>Additional Considerations</li><li>Arrhythmia prevention: Do not use prophylactic lidocaine or other antiarrhythmics to prevent VT/VF.</li><li>Electrolytes: Correct hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia to reduce arrhythmia risk.</li><li>Secondary prevention: Initiate high-intensity statin therapy in ACS patients unless contraindicated, regardless of baseline LDL-C.</li></ul>",
"85":"<ul><li>References:</li><li>1. Dyslipidemia, ASCVD prevention, and risk modification</li><li>Blumenthal RS, Morris PB, Gaudino M, et al. 2026 ACC/AHA/AACVPR/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Dyslipidemia. Circulation. 2026;153(17):e1154-e1276. doi:10.1161/CIR.0000000000001423.</li><li>2. PREVENT-ASCVD risk equations</li><li>Khan SS, Matsushita K, Sang Y, et al. Development and validation of the American Heart Association's PREVENT equations. Circulation. 2024;149(6):430-449. doi:10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.123.067626.</li><li>3. Chronic coronary disease and stable angina</li><li>Virani SS, Newby LK, Arnold SV, et al. 2023 AHA/ACC/ACCP/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease. Circulation. 2023;148(9):e9-e119. doi:10.1161/CIR.0000000000001168.</li></ul>",
"86":"<ul><li>References:</li><li>4. Chest pain evaluation and diagnostic testing</li><li>Gulati M, Levy PD, Mukherjee D, et al. 2021 AHA/ACC/ASE/CHEST/SAEM/SCCT/SCMR Guideline for the Evaluation and Diagnosis of Chest Pain. Circulation. 2021;144(22):e368-e454. doi:10.1161/CIR.0000000000001029.</li><li>5. Acute coronary syndrome management</li><li>Rao SV, O'Donoghue ML, Ruel M, et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes. Circulation. 2025;151(13):e771-e862. doi:10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309</li></ul>",
"87":"<ul><li>References:</li><li>6. Myocardial infarction definition and classification</li><li>Mills NL, Newby LK, Zaman S, et al. Fifth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction (2026). Circulation. Published online August 27, 2026. doi:10.1161/CIR.0000000000001477.</li><li>7. Exercise testing and physical activity</li><li>Fletcher GF, Balady GJ, Amsterdam EA, et al. Exercise standards for testing and training: a statement for healthcare professionals from the American Heart Association. Circulation. 2001;104(14):1694-1740. doi:10.1161/hc3901.095960</li></ul>"};
